  "summary": "Recent preprints and one publication treat molecular structure as architectural machinery across drug response, interaction, and omics tasks.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that structural information enters these models as architectural components, governing pairing, relational, or fusion operations rather than serving only as static input descriptors. GMJRL fuses network-level representations with atomic-geometry features for drug-target interaction prediction, while engGNN couples external biological networks with tree-ensemble-derived feature graphs for omics disease classification and feature selection. DiSPA applies differential cross-attention between chemical substructures and pathway gene expression to model bidirectional interactions for drug response, and GenRel-DDI recasts drug-drug interaction prediction as relation-centric learning that generalizes to unseen drugs on entity-disjoint benchmarks. What is new is the joint modeling of atomic, molecular, and interaction-network scales within a single architecture. The tasks and data modalities differ across studies, so the shared principle is architectural rather than uniform in implementation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
